-- title:
--   Regularity of dlog g when all orders are p-divisible
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra satisfying [`AlgebraicCurve.IsCurveOver K F`](def/AlgebraicCurve_IsCurveOver.html#L15): every nonzero $f \in F$ has a divisor of degree zero whose value at each place is $\operatorname{ord}_v f$, each residue field of a place is finite-dimensional over $K$, and the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega[F/K]$ is free of rank one over $F$. Here a place is a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, different from $F$ itself, whose ideals are principal, and $\operatorname{ord}_v f$ is minus the logarithm of the associated $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued adic valuation of $f$. Let $p$ be a prime with $K$ of characteristic $p$ and $K$ perfect, and let $x \in F$ be such that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$. Assume $g \in F$ satisfies $(p : \mathbb{Z}) \mid \operatorname{ord}_v g$ for every place $v$ of $F/K$ (the case $g = 0$ is not excluded). Then the logarithmic differential $g^{-1} \cdot D_{K,F}(g)$ is a regular differential: for every place $v$, the order $\operatorname{ord}_v$ of the coefficient of this form with respect to $D_{K,F}$ of the chosen uniformiser at $v$ is non-negative.
--
--   This is the local regularity criterion for logarithmic differentials in characteristic $p$: an element whose order at every place is divisible by $p$ is, locally at each place, a unit times a $p$-th power, so its logarithmic differential has no pole. It feeds the Cartier-operator description of the image of the degree-zero divisor class group, being cited by [`AlgebraicCurve.Pic0.range_eq_setOf_cartier_fixed_and_isRegularDiff`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.Pic0.range_eq_setOf_cartier_fixed_and_isRegularDiff) and by [`AlgebraicCurve.finite_and_card_torsion_le_pow_finrank`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.finite_and_card_torsion_le_pow_finrank).
